\f2 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 What Defence is called\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 The manuscript currently uses simply 
\f1\b Defence
\f0\b0  in dialogue and narration, and that actually works very well:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Defence estimates...\uc0\u8232 The Air Force...\uc0\u8232 Defence will provide a report...\uc0\u8232 Defence leaned forward...\
It feels like an institution so familiar that nobody needs to say its full title.\
There is an old synopsis using 
\f1\b Ulyssian Defence Force
\f0\b0 , but I would regard that as a placeholder rather than established terminology.\
If you want a full formal name, I would keep it boringly plausible:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Ulyssian Defence Force \'97 UDF
\f0\b0 \
with 
\f1\b Defence
\f0\b0  as the normal usage.\
I would 
\f1\b not
\f0\b0  manufacture an elaborate funny acronym for the organisation itself. Your existing institutional comedy is better than that.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 What people call them\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 The good nickname should come from their 
\f1\b record
\f0\b0 , because Defence already has one.\
They have:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 lost aircraft to hospitality clones;\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 had Elvi parachute into St Hedges;\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ordered tanks that were manufactured but somehow never delivered;\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 subsequently discovered those tanks being used by the Elvi;\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 been forced to explain all this to Dunhilton.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 That suggests two useful levels of nickname.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Official/patriotic:
\f0\b0  something descended from the old military mythology of Ulyssia. You already have the historical 
\f1\b UNSACs
\f0\b0 , the Ulyssia and New Sheepland Army Corps. I would strongly consider letting modern Defence cultivate the old UNSAC tradition in exactly the same way Australia trades heavily on ANZAC imagery. That gives Defence ceremonial gravitas without inventing another institution.\

\f1\b Popular/derisive:
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the Lawn Rangers
\f0\b0 .\
That one strikes me as particularly useful in this world. They are supposedly national defence forces, but what the Cloudborne mostly see them doing is defending Airborne property, mansions, hover-pads, shareholder precincts and now Eloise Shakti's extremely expensive lawn.\
It can exist without becoming a running gag every time Defence appears:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Defence had deployed two hundred Lawn Rangers...\strokec2 \
or an Elvi:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93Lawn Rangers are here.\'94\strokec2 \
Someone in Defence would hate the name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 What they are laughed at for\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 I would make their institutional comic identity:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Defence is lavishly equipped, conventionally trained, politically abused and repeatedly defeated by threats omitted from the procurement assumptions.
\f0\b0 \
That is already what the manuscript is doing.\
Their problem isn't that they're cowardly or stupid soldiers. That would flatten them. The institution has prepared extremely well for the wars its contractors sold it.\
Then it encounters:\

\f1\b hospitality clones flying obsolete aircraft
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Elvi with stolen tanks
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\b couches occupied by shareholders' children
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\b stage amplifiers
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Gyges discs thrown like dangerous rubbish
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Fridge Force
\f0\b0 \
And every time somebody asks why this possibility wasn't covered by the threat model.\
That gives you a Defence joke with much more longevity than \'93their acronym sounds rude.\'94\
There could even be a recognised bureaucratic expression such as 
\f1\b an unmodelled threat
\f0\b0 , because Defence's great historical excuse is effectively:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 nobody modelled that.\strokec2 \
The existing tank exchange is already very close to defining this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93Every contingency\'97air raids, hackers, chem-bio, circus troupes\'97nobody ran a model for tanks.\'94\strokec2 \
followed by the revelation that they were 
\f1\b Defence's own tanks
\f0\b0 .\
That's their comic soul.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_H::3>
\f1\b\fs28 The force at Sublime Point specifically\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f0 I wouldn't deploy the entire UDF. I'd make it something like a 
\f1\b Defence task group
\f0\b0 , probably built around a ground battalion with engineering, signals and air support.\
Normal narration could therefore move naturally between:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Defence had surrounded Sublime Point.
\f0\b0 \
and, when specificity matters:\

\f1\b the Defence task group
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b the infantry
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b the APC crews
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b the Air Force hover-copts
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b the TRAMPS checkpoint
\f0\b0 \
That also solves Alexander's production-truck problem nicely. His convoy doesn't somehow pass through \'93the army.\'94 It reaches a 
\f1\b TRAMPS-controlled outer checkpoint
\f0\b0 , where the police have jurisdiction over access and can wave him through, while Defence personnel farther in quite reasonably wonder why a television outside-broadcast truck has just entered their siege.\
I would therefore revise the conceptual board from:\

\f1\b Government forces vs Shakti vs Elvi vs Fridge Force
\f0\b0 \
to:\

\f1\b Defence
\f0\b0  \'97 conventional siege force\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b TRAMPS
\f0\b0  \'97 perimeter/police authority\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b PAPER/Gerry
\f0\b0  \'97 covert agenda\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Shakti
\f0\b0  \'97 compound security\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Elvi
\f0\b0  \'97 irregular intervention\uc0\u8232 
\f1\b Fridge Force
\f0\b0  \'97 specialist technological intervention\
That gives each institution something different to be good at, and something different to be ridiculous about.\
